{"schema": "workere-benchmark-suite/v1", "protocol": "workere-historical-relations/v1", "config": {"seed": 42, "active_dataset": "causal_timebank", "datasets": {"causal_timebank": {"name": "CausalTimeBank-standard-eci", "repo_id": "Nofing/CausalTimeBank-standard-eci-workere", "split": "train", "text_field": "text", "ann_field": "annots", "max_examples": 0, "kfold": {"enabled": true, "n_folds": 10}, "constrain_to_pair_list": false}}, "data": {"shuffle_docs": true, "shuffle_pair_list": true, "reshuffle_per_resample": true, "binary_undirected": false, "vote_to_binary": false, "binary_default": "norel", "novote_norel": true, "labels": ["causes", "causedby", "norel"]}, "experiment": {"resampling": {"enabled": true, "n_runs": 7, "tie_breaking": "norel", "distinct_fewshots": true}}}, "documents": [{"id": "wsj_1013", "idx": 172, "text": "Columbia Savings amp Loan Association , <ei1 reeling> from thrift-accounting <ei3 changes> <ei4 mandated> by Congress and the recent <ei5 collapse> of the junk-bond market , <ei6 announced> a <ei7 loss> for the third quarter of $ 226.3 million , or $ 11.57 a share . For the quarter a year ago , Columbia <ei10 reported> earnings of $ 16.3 million , or 37 cents a share . Total assets <ei11 increased> to $ 12.7 billion in the latest quarter from $ 12.4 billion a year earlier . The <ei14 loss> <ei15 stems> from $ 357.5 million of <ei16 write-downs> on Columbia 's $ 4.4 billion high-yield investment securities portfolio , which includes about $ 3.7 billion of junk bonds , $ 400 million of preferred stock , and Treasury securities . Columbia <ei17 owes> its spectacular <ei18 growth> in recent years to its junk-bond portfolio , the largest of any U.S. thrift . Much of Columbia 's junk-bond <ei20 trading> has been <ei21 done> through the high-yield department of its Beverly Hills neighbor , Drexel Burnham Lambert Inc. For the nine months , losses <ei23 totaled> $ 212 million , or $ 10.83 a share , compared with net income of $ 48.7 million , or $ 1.11 a share , a year earlier . The results include a $ 130.2 million write-down of the securities in the high-yield portfolio to the lower of their cost or market value . Columbia also <ei25 added> $ 227.3 million to reserves for <ei26 losses> on the portfolio , <ei27 increasing> general reserves to $ 300 million , or about 6.7 % of the total portfolio , as of Sept . 30 . On June 30 , loss reserves <ei30 stood> at $ 108.3 million . Thrift officials <ei31 said> the $ 300 million reserve will be <ei32 adjusted> quarterly and will <ei33 reflect> the rate of dispositions and market conditions . The adjustments <ei34 result> from the recently <ei35 passed> thrift-industry bailout legislation , which <ei36 requires> thrifts to <ei37 divest> all high-yield bond investments by 1994 . Previously , Columbia did n't have to <ei39 adjust> the book value of its junk-bond holdings to <ei40 reflect> <ei41 declines> in market prices , because it <ei43 held> the bonds as long-term investments . Because Columbia now must <ei46 sell> the bonds within five years , accounting rules <ei48 require> the thrift to <ei49 value> the bonds at the lower of cost or market prices . For its future strategy , Columbia officials <ei50 said> the thrift may <ei51 branch> out into commercial lending or managing outside investments , as well as <ei52 beefing> up more traditional thrift activities . The quarterly results also <ei53 reflected> $ 21.4 million in non-recurring losses from commercial real-estate activities in California . Thomas Spiegel , Columbia 's chairman , <ei54 said> in a statement that the thrift was \" <ei55 disappointed> \" by the effects of the accounting <ei56 changes> . But he <ei57 said> Columbia <ei58 remains> \" one of the most strongly capitalized thrifts in the industry , \" based on the economic value of its assets and tangible capital . Columbia <ei59 announced> the results after the <ei60 close> of the stock market . Its shares <ei61 closed> at $ 5.125 each in composite New York Stock Exchange trading , down 37.5 cents . The price of Columbia shares has been <ei62 cut> nearly in half since August , when they <ei64 traded> at about $ 10 , as investors apparently <ei66 realized> that the thrift would be <ei67 forced> to <ei68 take> a big write-down . The stock 's <ei69 decline> <ei70 accelerated> in the past two weeks , from a price of $ 8 a share on Oct . 9 . Columbia officials <ei73 said> they do n't <ei74 know> how quickly they will <ei75 dispose> of the thrift 's junk bonds , because federal regulations , such as those that would <ei77 allow> thrifts to <ei78 continue> <ei79 holding> the bonds in separately capitalized subsidiaries , have n't yet been <ei80 completed> . Columbia officials also <ei81 said> the thrift should n't <ei82 face> problems meeting regulatory capital requirements , despite the large reserves and write-downs and stiffer regulatory requirements that should be in place by year 's end . Its ratio of tangible equity to total assets as of Sept . 30 was 3.6 % , and total equity was $ 457.9 million . The thrift <ei85 emphasized> that it <ei86 has> a large portfolio of equity securities <ei87 issued> in connection with corporate restructurings and leveraged <ei88 buy-outs> , which <ei89 has> a book value of $ 90 million . Although many of the transactions related to those securities have n't been <ei90 completed> , Columbia <ei91 said> the ultimate gain on the <ei92 sale> of those assets will <ei93 range> from $ 200 million to $ 300 million . Columbia also <ei94 has> unrealized gains in its public equity securities portfolio of more than $ 70 million . David B. Hilder in New York <ei95 contributed> to this article .", "gold": [["ei3", "causes", "ei1"], ["ei16", "causes", "ei14"], ["ei43", "causes", "ei39"], ["ei46", "causes", "ei48"], ["ei66", "causes", "ei62"], ["ei80", "causes", "ei74"], ["ei1", "causedby", "ei3"], ["ei14", "causedby", "ei16"], ["ei39", "causedby", "ei43"], ["ei48", "causedby", "ei46"], ["ei62", "